		<dc:title id="title">The Fall of Robespierre</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#title">Fall of Robespierre, The</meta>
		<meta property="title-type" refines="#title">main</meta>
		<dc:title id="subtitle">An Historic Drama</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#subtitle">Historic Drama, An</meta>
		<meta property="title-type" refines="#subtitle">subtitle</meta>
		<dc:title id="fulltitle">The Fall of Robespierre: An Historic Drama</dc:title>
		<meta property="file-as" refines="#fulltitle">The Fall of Robespierre</meta>
		<meta property="title-type" refines="#fulltitle">expanded</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-1">English poetry -- 19th century</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-1">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-1">sh85043937</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Drama</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">A poetic drama depicting the final days of Maximilien Robespierre.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;Maximilien Robespierre has risen to prominence within the new French National Convention. A radical Jacobin, he oversaw the draconian policies of the “Reign of Terror,” including mass arrests and summary executions. Increasingly, he fears a conspiracy is afoot, which will eventually turn the Convention, and the very policies he championed, against him and his allies.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Set during the final days of Maximilien Robespierre, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/samuel-taylor-coleridge"&gt;Samuel Taylor Coleridge&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/robert-southey"&gt;Robert Southey&lt;/a&gt; wrote this poetic drama less than three months after Robespierre was executed at the guillotine in the Place de la Révolution.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
